### 5\. Code Explanation Line by Line

1\. Header files

* `#include <iostream>` – Includes the input and output library used for `cin` and `cout`.

* `#include <algorithm>` – Includes the algorithm library, which provides the `max()` function.

* `using namespace std;` – Allows us to use standard library functions without writing `std::` every time.

2\. Main function and variable declaration

* `int main()` – The main function where program execution begins.

* `int n, W;` – Declares two integer variables. `n` stores the number of items and `W` stores the knapsack capacity.

3\. Accepting the number of items

* `cout << "Enter number of items: ";` – Displays a message asking the user to enter the number of items.

* `cin >> n;` – Accepts the number of items from the user.

4\. Declaring arrays

\- `int weight[n], value[n];` – Creates two arrays. The weight array stores the weights of items, while the value array stores their corresponding profits.

5\. Accepting item weights

* `cout << "Enter weights of items:\n";` – Displays a message to enter item weights.

* `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` – Runs a loop from 0 to n-1 to accept the weight of every item.

* `cin >> weight[i];` – Stores each item's weight in the weight array.

6\. Accepting item values

* `cout << "Enter values of items:\n";` – Displays a message to enter the profit or value of each item.

* `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` – Iterates through all the items.

* `cin >> value[i];` – Stores the value of each item in the value array.

7\. Accepting knapsack capacity

* `cout << "Enter knapsack capacity: ";` – Asks the user to enter the maximum weight capacity.

* `cin >> W;` – Stores the maximum capacity in variable `W`.

8\. Creating the DP table

\- `int dp[n + 1][W + 1];` – Creates a two-dimensional DP table to store the maximum profit for different numbers of items and capacities.

9\. Initializing the DP table

* `for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)` – Iterates through all item rows.

* `for (int w = 0; w <= W; w++)` – Iterates through all capacity columns.

* `if (i == 0 || w == 0)` – Checks whether there are no items or the capacity is zero.

* `dp[i][w] = 0;` – Assigns zero profit in either base case.

10\. Checking whether an item can be included

\- `else if (weight[i - 1] <= w)` – Checks whether the current item's weight is less than or equal to the available capacity.

11\. Calculating maximum profit

* `value[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][w - weight[i - 1]]` – Calculates the profit when the current item is included.

* `dp[i - 1][w]` – Represents the profit when the current item is excluded.

* `max(...)` – Selects the greater profit between including and excluding the item.

* `dp[i][w] = ...` – Stores the maximum profit in the DP table.

12\. When an item cannot be included

* `else` – Executes when the item's weight is greater than the available capacity.

* `dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w];` – Copies the maximum profit from the previous row because the current item cannot be selected.

13\. Displaying maximum profit

\- `cout << "\nMaximum Profit: " << dp[n][W] << endl;` – Displays the maximum profit stored in the last cell of the DP table.

14\. Finding selected items

* `cout << "Selected Items: ";` – Displays a heading for selected items.

* `int w = W;` – Initializes the current capacity to the maximum knapsack capacity.

* `for (int i = n; i > 0; i--)` – Traverses the DP table backwards to identify selected items.

* `if (dp[i][w] != dp[i - 1][w])` – Checks whether the current item was included in the optimal solution.

* `cout << i << " ";` – Displays the selected item's number.

* `w = w - weight[i - 1];` – Reduces the remaining capacity by the weight of the selected item.

15\. Ending the program

\- `return 0;` – Terminates the program successfully.

### 6\. Conclusion

The 0/1 Knapsack problem was successfully implemented using Dynamic Programming in C++. The program calculates the maximum possible profit by selecting items without exceeding the given knapsack capacity. A two-dimensional DP table is used to store the results of smaller subproblems and avoid repeated calculations. The selected items are also identified by tracing back through the DP table.

Thus, Dynamic Programming provides an efficient approach to solving the 0/1 Knapsack optimization problem.

